          <SocialProof users="12.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#intervals" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Intervals by Sealer Type</a>
          <a href="#what-drives-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Changes the Interval</a>
          <a href="#signs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Signs It Is Due</a>
          <a href="#too-often" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Risk of Resealing Too Often</a>
          <a href="#process" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Resealing Properly</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cost Over Time</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most guidance gives a number and stops there. The number is a starting point — what actually determines your interval is the sealer chemistry, how much sun the surface takes, and whether anyone parks on it.
        </p>

        <div id="intervals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Often to Reseal, by Sealer Type
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easier to apply and recoat; softer film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 to 5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harder film, stronger colour enhancement</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating (silane, siloxane)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 to 6 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No surface film to wear; less visual change</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet look, high gloss</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wear shows most obviously in gloss finishes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint stabilising sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Binds joint sand as well as coating the surface</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers last longest because there is no film on the surface for traffic to wear away — the protection sits inside the pore structure of the stone. The trade is that they change the appearance very little, so if you sealed for the wet-look colour enhancement rather than for stain resistance, a penetrating product is not what you want.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Interval Is a Range for a Reason</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Two identical patios can be two years apart.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A shaded, lightly used path with a solvent-based sealer might comfortably reach six years. A south-facing driveway with a wet-look finish and two cars on it may need attention at two. Use the ranges to plan and the surface itself to decide.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-drives-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Changes Your Interval
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sun Exposure</h3>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light degrades sealer films, and it does so relentlessly on an unshaded surface. A south-facing patio in full sun will need resealing meaningfully sooner than a shaded courtyard with the identical product. It is usually the single largest factor after sealer chemistry, and it is why one end of a long driveway often looks ready before the other.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Traffic Type</h3>

          <p>
            Foot traffic wears sealer slowly. Vehicles wear it quickly, and turning vehicles quickest of all. The pattern on a sealed driveway makes this obvious: the wheel tracks and the turning area dull first while the margins still look freshly sealed. Where a driveway and a patio share the same product and the same age, the driveway will always be due first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Climate and Freeze-Thaw</h3>

          <p>
            Repeated freezing and thawing stresses any surface film, and water that has found its way beneath one expands and lifts it. Regions with many crossings of the freezing point through winter are harder on sealers than consistently cold or consistently mild ones, because it is the cycling rather than the cold itself that does the damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Winter Maintenance</h3>

          <p>
            De-icing salt is corrosive and works into any breach in a sealer film, and a metal snow shovel or a plough blade scrapes it off in strips. A single hard winter of aggressive snow clearing can take more life out of a sealed surface than two ordinary years of use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Well It Was Applied</h3>

          <p>
            As with most coatings, the application decides much of the lifespan. Sealer applied to a properly cleaned, fully dry surface at the right coverage rate lasts near the top of its range. Sealer applied over dirt, efflorescence or residual moisture fails early regardless of what it cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Tell Your Pavers Are Due for Resealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Let the surface decide rather than the calendar. These are the signals, in rough order of how early they appear.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The finish has dulled and lost the sheen it had when new, particularly in traffic paths.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Water no longer beads on the surface and instead soaks in and darkens the paver.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Colour looks flatter and less saturated than it did, especially on a wet-look finish.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Stains are starting to hold rather than wiping away — the clearest functional signal.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Traffic areas look visibly different from protected margins under furniture or planters.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Moss or algae is beginning to establish in areas that previously stayed clear.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Water Test</h3>

          <p>
            The single most useful check. Pour a cup of water onto a paver in the main traffic area. On a sound sealed surface it beads and sits. On a worn one it darkens the stone within seconds as it soaks in. Since stain resistance is the main functional reason to seal, water absorption is exactly the right thing to measure — and it is far more reliable than judging appearance, which varies with light and weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test in several places. A patio frequently wears unevenly, and finding that the sheltered corner still beads while the main walkway does not tells you the surface is due even though it may still look acceptable overall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Resealing Too Often Causes Problems
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part that gets left out of most maintenance advice, and it matters because the instinct — reseal annually to be safe — actively damages a sealed surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Film Build-Up</h3>

          <p>
            Each coat of a film-forming sealer applied before the previous one has worn thin adds to a stack. Past a certain thickness the accumulated film cannot flex with the stone through thermal cycling and it cracks, then lifts. The result looks like flaking or peeling and it is entirely self-inflicted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clouding and Blushing</h3>

          <p>
            Thicker films are also more prone to trapping moisture, and trapped moisture beneath a sealer film is what produces the whitish, cloudy appearance people find so frustrating. Each additional coat increases both the film thickness and the chance of sealing over a surface that had not fully dried.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An Increasingly Glossy, Artificial Look</h3>

          <p>
            Successive coats deepen the sheen, and a patio sealed every year for five years often looks noticeably plasticky compared with one sealed twice in the same period. It is a matter of taste, but it is rarely the look people intended when they started.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Right Rhythm</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Reseal when water stops beading, not when the calendar says so.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A surface that still repels water does not need another coat, however long it has been. Waiting for the water test to fail is what keeps the film thin enough to stay flexible, and it is the same principle that governs driveway sealer and most other maintenance coatings.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="process" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Reseal Without Causing Problems
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Confirm it is actually due</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Run the water test in several places, including the busiest area and a sheltered one. If water still beads across the surface, wait.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Check whether the old sealer is sound</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Flaking, peeling or heavy clouding means stripping rather than recoating. A new coat over a failing film fails with it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clean thoroughly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Remove dirt, organic growth, efflorescence and any stains. Whatever is on the surface gets sealed under the new coat permanently.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Top up the joints if they have dropped</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Resealing is the natural moment to restore eroded joint sand, and it needs its own cure time before sealing.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Let everything dry completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A full dry day after cleaning, longer in cool or humid weather. Joints hold moisture far longer than the surface looks.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Match the sealer to what is already there</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Water-based over solvent-based, or vice versa, can react badly. Use the same type, or strip and start fresh if you want to change.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Apply thin and even</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">One properly applied coat at the recommended coverage rate. Resist the urge to lay it on heavily for a deeper look.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Respect the cure before use</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Follow the product’s traffic timings, and keep vehicles off longer than foot traffic.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That sixth step catches people out more than any other. Sealers are not universally compatible, and applying a solvent-based product over a water-based one — or the reverse — can soften, lift or cloud the existing film. If you do not know what is on the pavers, test a small inconspicuous area and wait a full day before committing to the whole surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Resealing Costs Over a Patio’s Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because sealing is a recurring commitment rather than a one-off, the sensible way to compare products is cost per year rather than cost per bottle.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Relative Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Relative Cost per Year</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest over time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet look / high gloss</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Wet-look finishes carry the highest running cost, because the look people buy them for is precisely what shows wear first. A high-gloss surface makes every dull patch obvious, so it gets recoated sooner than a matte finish would with the same actual wear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Labour Is the Real Cost</h3>

          <p>
            For most homeowners the product is not the expensive part. Clearing the patio of furniture and planters, cleaning it thoroughly, waiting a day for it to dry, and then applying the sealer is most of a weekend each time. A product that buys two extra years between cycles is worth considerably more than its price difference suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            That argument points toward penetrating sealers for anyone whose priority is protection rather than appearance, and toward accepting a matte or natural-look film-forming product over a high gloss if you want colour enhancement without the shortest possible interval.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing on an Annual Schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Applying a fresh coat before the previous one has worn thin stacks film that eventually cannot flex with the stone, and it cracks and lifts. A patio sealed every year for five years is usually in worse condition than one sealed twice. Let the water test decide, not the calendar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Over a Failing Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new coat bonds to the old film, so if that film is flaking, peeling or heavily clouded, the new sealer lifts along with it. Failing sealer needs stripping before anything goes on top. Recoating only works over a sound, intact film that has simply worn thin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Incompatible Sealer Types</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent-based products applied over water-based ones, or the reverse, can soften, lift or cloud the existing film. If you do not know what is already on the pavers, test an inconspicuous area and wait a full day before committing the whole surface to a product that may react with it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Dirt or Efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Anything left on the surface when the sealer goes down is locked under the film permanently, and efflorescence in particular becomes a white bloom you cannot reach. Clean thoroughly, treat any efflorescence, and only then seal — the cleaning step is what determines how the finish looks for the next several years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the Same Day You Cleaned</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Washing puts water into the pavers and deep into the joints, and both hold it far longer than the surface appearance suggests. Sealing over residual moisture traps it beneath the film and produces the cloudy blush that people then spend a weekend trying to remove. Allow a full dry day, longer if it is cool or humid.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should pavers be resealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically every two to three years for water-based acrylics, three to five for solvent-based, and four to six for penetrating sealers. Those are starting points — sun exposure, vehicle traffic and winter salt all shorten them, while shade and light use extend them. Use the water test rather than the calendar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when pavers need resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pour a cup of water on a paver in the main traffic area. If it beads and sits, the sealer is still working. If it soaks in and darkens the stone within seconds, the surface is due. Dulling, flatter colour and stains that no longer wipe away are the supporting signals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal pavers too often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it causes real problems. Each coat applied before the previous has worn thin builds film that eventually cannot flex with the stone, so it cracks and peels. Thicker films are also more likely to trap moisture and cloud. Annual resealing typically leaves a patio in worse condition than a two to three year cycle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to strip old sealer before resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is failing. A sound film that has simply worn thin can be cleaned and recoated. Flaking, peeling or heavily clouded sealer must come off first, because a new coat bonds to the old film and lifts with it. Incompatible chemistry is the other reason to strip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do penetrating paver sealers last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically four to six years, longer than film-forming sealers because the protection sits inside the pore structure of the stone where traffic cannot wear it away. The trade-off is that they change the appearance very little, so they are not the choice if you sealed for wet-look colour enhancement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a sealed driveway need resealing more often than a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, usually. Vehicle traffic wears sealer far faster than foot traffic, and turning wheels fastest of all. On a driveway the wheel tracks and turning area dull well before the margins. Where a driveway and patio share the same product and age, the driveway will always come due first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I reseal only part of a paver patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but the repaired area will usually differ in sheen from the rest for some time, and the boundary tends to stay visible. Sectional resealing makes sense on a driveway where wheel tracks wear far faster than the margins; on a patio, doing the whole surface generally looks better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I reseal before or after winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Before, if conditions allow. A sound sealer film protects against de-icing salt and freeze-thaw damage through the season when the surface is most vulnerable. Just make sure the product has its full cure window in reasonable temperatures before the weather turns.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

