        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plan on two to three years for a water-based acrylic, three to five for solvent-based, and four to six for a penetrating sealer — then let the surface rather than the calendar make the final call. A south-facing driveway with cars on it and a shaded courtyard path can be years apart on identical products.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The water test settles it. Pour a cup on a paver in the busiest area: beading means the sealer is still doing its job whatever the date, soaking in means it is due. Since stain resistance is the main reason to seal in the first place, water absorption is precisely the right thing to be measuring.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And resist the urge to reseal early to be safe. Every coat applied over a film that has not worn thin adds thickness the surface eventually cannot flex with, and it cracks, clouds and peels. Resealing too often is a more common cause of ugly pavers than resealing too late.
          </p>
        </div>

